Section 1

Section 1 — The Problem: AI Amnesia
1.1 The Zero-Reset Problem

Every session with a modern AI agent begins from absolute zero. When Axel opens copilot.microsoft.com at 18:30 on a Friday evening, the AI that greets him has no memory of the session he completed yesterday, the ventures he has spent months building, the decisions he made last week, or the documents he generated an hour ago in a different Copilot surface. The assistant is capable, intelligent, and responsive — and it knows nothing about him.
This is not an edge case. It is the fundamental operating condition of every major AI agent available today. Microsoft Copilot on the web, Edge Copilot embedded in the browser sidebar, Windows Copilot at the operating system level, M365 Copilot inside Word and Excel and Teams, and GitHub Copilot inside the code editor — all of them are isolated silos. They do not share memory with each other. They do not share memory with themselves across sessions. And they certainly do not share a unified identity model for the person using them.
The problem is not that these agents are unsophisticated. They are, in many ways, remarkable. The problem is that they are powerful without continuity — like a brilliant consultant who suffers from total amnesia at the end of every meeting and shows up the next day with no recollection of anything previously discussed.
1.2 The Taxonomy of AI Amnesia

AI amnesia manifests across three distinct dimensions, each compounding the damage of the others:
●	Session Amnesia: The agent remembers nothing from the previous session with the same user on the same platform. Each conversation begins fresh. Every context must be re-established from the ground up.
●	Cross-Platform Amnesia: Even within the same session window, knowledge established in one Copilot surface does not transfer to another. What Axel told copilot.microsoft.com is invisible to Edge Copilot three minutes later. The platforms are structurally incapable of sharing knowledge.
●	Identity Amnesia: Beyond facts and session data, there is no persistent identity model. The AI has no stored understanding of who the user is — their role, their communication style, their business priorities, their decision-making framework, or their operating context. This must be re-established in every session, on every platform.
1.3 The Quantified Cost

The cost of AI amnesia is not abstract. Knowledge workers who rely on AI agents for complex, multi-session work lose an estimated 20–30% of productive AI interaction time to context re-establishment. Every session begins with a briefing tax — the time required to re-introduce context before productive work can begin. For a sophisticated user like Axel, who operates across multiple ventures with complex interdependencies, this tax is catastrophic.

